      "textEn": "493\n\nTam—that; Atmanah—self; Anugraha-Artham—to show grace; Preetya—being overly pleased; Matsya-Vapuh—one who holds the body of a fish; Ajanan—without knowing; Rakshana-Arthaya—for protection; Shapharyah—of the fish; Saha—that king; Manah—in the mind; Dadhe—decided. King Satyavrat, unknowing that this fish is God, happily\ndecided to provide protection to that fish. Meaning: Here, an example of serving God without knowing is presented. Such service is unknown-\nis called culture. King Satyavrat wanted to show his mercy; he did not know that this fish\nis Lord Vishnu. Such unknown devotion brings the grace of God. Whether the service to God is known or unknown,\nit never goes in vain. Then, hearing the words of humility, the king.\nKalashapshu nidhayainam dayalur ninya ashramam. 16. Meaning of the word\n\nTasyaha - of that fish; Din-taram - extremely compassionate; Vakyam - to the word; Ashritya - having heard; Saha - that; Mahi-patih - King; Kalash-apsu - in the water kept in the pot; Nidhaya - having kept; Anaam - this fish; Dayaluha - compassionate; Ninye - brought;\nAshramam - one's home..\n\nBeing influenced by the compassionate words of that fish, that kind king put that fish in a\nwater vessel and brought it to his home. That which increases with one night of the moon. I am telling you this, O King, that the soul is unavailable. 17. Meaning of the word\n\nSa—that fish; Tu—but; Tatra—there; Ek-raterna—in one night; Vardhamana—increasing; Kamandalau—in a water vessel;\nAlabdhva—not finding; Atma-avakasham—a convenient place for one's body; Va—or; Idam—this; Aah—where;\nMahi-patim—from the king..\nBut the fish grew so big in one night that it started having difficulty in moving its body here and there in that vessel. Then he said to the king in this way. I do not desire any object, which is not desired by me.\nI live in a beautiful place where I find plenty of happiness. 1. Lexicon"
